## 4.3 Authentication backend

Reuse and extend the existing magic-link authentication architecture.

Implement this complete process:

1. User enters email.
2. Frontend validates the request.
3. Backend receives the authentication request.
4. Backend securely creates a time-limited, single-use token.
5. Resend sends the authentication email.
6. User opens the magic link.
7. Backend verifies the token.
8. Token is invalidated after successful use.
9. User session is securely established.
10. User reaches the dashboard.
11. Cloud synchronisation becomes available.

Implement:

- Token expiration.
- Single-use tokens.
- Rate limiting.
- Secure cookies or an appropriately designed session mechanism.
- Server-side authorization.
- Session expiration.
- Logout and session revocation.

Never expose API keys, authentication secrets or Resend credentials in frontend code.

Do not fake login success.

Do not claim that an email has been sent unless the email service confirms the operation.

When production credentials are missing, show a clear configuration message and preserve guest mode.

Provide a development-only demo authentication mode, isolated from production authentication.

